        "learnerExperience": "The full Driving Question Board — built across Lessons 1–7 — is displayed at the front of the room (or projected if digital) (9 minutes). Groups spend 3 minutes reviewing every question still marked as 'unanswered' or 'partially answered' on the DQB, select one, agree a consensus answer and write it on a sticky note to be placed on the board. The teacher then reads aloud the unit driving question (1 minute): 'How can a Kericho tea farmer — or any Kenyan scientist, engineer, or business person — use the mathematics of indices and logarithms to make sense of, and calculate with, numbers that are too large or too small to handle by ordinary arithmetic?' Each learner then independently writes a structured 'Final Explanation' of 5–6 sentences in their exercise book (5 minutes). The response must: (i) reference the Kericho phenomenon explicitly; (ii) explain index notation; (iii) explain how logarithms convert multiplication to addition; (iv) give one specific example using numbers from the unit; (v) state why this matters for a Kenyan scientist, engineer, or business person.",
        "teacherMoves": "Uncover the full DQB and say: 'This board has been building since Lesson 1. Every question your class asked is here. Your groups have 3 minutes — find a question still unanswered, agree on your answer, and post a sticky note. Go.' [Circulate and prompt groups who are stuck: 'Can you use something from your concept map to answer that?' / 'Does Problem A from today's worksheet give you evidence for that question?'] After posting: read 2 sticky notes aloud. Say: 'Look at this board. Every question you asked eight lessons ago — you can now answer. That is what learning looks like.' Read the driving question aloud. Then say: 'Now I want your final explanation in writing. Alone. No talking. 5 minutes. You must mention the tea farmer, index notation, logarithm laws, a specific number, and a Kenyan application. Begin.' Collect all written final explanations as a summative formative artefact.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "DQB Closure with Written Final Explanation — the DQB walkthrough provides collective validation that the unit's epistemic goals have been achieved, transforming learner-generated questions into learner-generated answers. The individual written explanation is a high-leverage sensemaking task: it requires synthesis across all eight lessons, explicit use of the phenomenon, and translation of procedural knowledge into communicative explanation. This directly mirrors NGSS SEP 6 (Constructing Explanations) and KICD's emphasis on applying mathematical knowledge to real-life situations.",
