    "teacherReflection": "After delivering this final lesson, reflect on the following questions: (1) Did learners' written Final Explanations demonstrate genuine understanding of why logarithms convert multiplication into addition, or did responses remain procedural ('you look up the number in the table')? If most responses were procedural, consider introducing the inverse-of-exponentiation framing earlier in a future unit cycle — perhaps as early as Lesson 4. (2) Were all DQB questions answered satisfactorily? Any question left genuinely unresolved points to a gap in the unit's lesson sequence — note it for the next cohort. (3) Did learners handle bar numbers in combined operations fluently, or did errors cluster around negative characteristics when applying the power law? If so, a dedicated 10-minute bar-number consolidation slot in Lesson 6 or 7 would reduce end-of-unit error rates. (4) Did the two-colour concept map revision successfully make learning growth visible to learners? If learners found it hard to distinguish new learning from prior knowledge, consider assigning a specific colour to each lesson from Lesson 1 onward. (5) Did the Kericho phenomenon feel authentically connected across all eight lessons, or did it become 'decoration' by the end? The phenomenon should be the engine of every phase — if it felt detached in this lesson, review the storyline thread language in Lessons 4–6 and strengthen the phenomenon callback in the teacher moves. (6) Equity check: which learners did not read their Final Explanation aloud? Ensure those learners are included in cold-call routines in future units. Note any learners scoring 'Below Expectations' on the KICD rubric for targeted support in subsequent sub-strands (particularly Quadratic Expressions and Equations, which builds on index fluency).",
